/*
=============================================================================
HOW THE CALCULATION WORKS
=============================================================================

GOAL
----
Given 24 hours (or 96 quarter-hours) of electricity prices from Tibber,
find the optimal battery charge/discharge schedule that maximises profit
(i.e. buy cheap, sell expensive) while respecting physical constraints.

INPUT
-----
- Tibber price data: one price per slot (EUR/kWh, incl. taxes)
- Battery capacity, min/max SOC, charge/discharge rates, efficiencies

BATTERY MODEL
-------------
- Energy is tracked in Wh (batt_min to batt_max).
- Charging: grid AC power drawn = setpoint (9000 W).
  Energy added to battery = power * charge_efficiency (e.g. 90.7%).
  Cost = (power / charge_efficiency / 1000) * price  [EUR]
- Discharging: AC power delivered = setpoint (9000 W).
  Energy removed from battery = power / discharge_efficiency (e.g. 89.6%).
  Earned = (power * discharge_efficiency / 1000) * price  [EUR]
- Idle: only house loads / inverter standby (110 W), no profit/cost tracked.
- Round-trip efficiency = charge_efficiency * discharge_efficiency
  (e.g. 90.7% * 89.6% = ~81.3%)

ALGORITHM: FORWARD DYNAMIC PROGRAMMING
---------------------------------------
The battery SOC is discretised into integer levels:
  level k  =>  SOC = min(batt_max, batt_min + k * calc_charge_rate)
where calc_charge_rate = batt_charge_rate * 0.25 (for quarter-hour slots).

State: dp[k] = best cumulative profit reachable with battery at level k
       after processing all slots up to the current one.

For each time slot, three actions are evaluated from every reachable level k:

  IDLE:      new level = k (unchanged)
             profit change = 0

  CHARGE:    new level = ceil((soc + power - batt_min) / calc_charge_rate)
             (ceil prevents energy-creation aliasing at top level)
             profit change = -(power / charge_efficiency / 1000) * price

  DISCHARGE: new level = floor((soc - power - batt_min) / calc_charge_rate)
             (floor pairs with ceil above to prevent aliasing)
             profit change = +(power * discharge_efficiency / 1000)
                              * (price - price_min_sell_difference)
             The threshold (price_min_sell_difference, e.g. 0.06 EUR/kWh)
             is subtracted from the price in the DP so that a discharge
             is only chosen when the sell price sufficiently exceeds the
             cost of the charge that filled the battery.

After processing all slots, the level with the highest dp[k] is chosen
as the end state, and the action sequence is recovered by backtracking
through the stored decisions.

POST-PROCESSING
---------------
After backtracking, two cleanups are applied:
1. Any charges scheduled after the last discharge are converted to idle
   (no point charging if there is nothing left to sell).
2. A final forward pass recomputes:
   - battery_capacity_watt  : actual Wh in battery each slot
   - battery_capacity_percentage : SOC %
   - earned_money           : cumulative EUR profit/loss
     Charges subtract (power / charge_efficiency / 1000) * price
     Discharges add  (power * discharge_efficiency / 1000) * price
   - setpoint               : Victron Multiplus AC setpoint (W)
     charge  => +batt_charge_rate
     discharge => -batt_discharge_rate
     idle    => idle_rate (110 W)

EARNED MONEY INTERPRETATION
----------------------------
earned_money represents the net profit from battery arbitrage only.
It does NOT include:
- Normal household electricity consumption
- Grid feed-in taxes or levies
- Battery degradation costs (partially covered by price_min_sell_difference)
A positive final value means the day's trading is profitable after
accounting for charge/discharge efficiency losses.

=============================================================================
Variables for testing purposes:


//From HomeBattGlobals:
context.global.batt_capacity = 62000; //Wh
context.global.batt_charge_efficiency = 0.907;   //90.7% AC->battery efficiency
context.global.batt_discharge_efficiency = 0.92; //92% battery->AC efficiency
context.global.number_of_multiplus = 3;
context.global.max_charge_setpoint = 9000; 
context.global.max_discharge_setpoint = 10000;
context.global.idle_rate = 103;
context.global.min_soc = 30;
context.global.max_soc = 100;
context.global.charge_rate = context.global.max_charge_setpoint; // used in batt control as charge setpoint for inverter
context.global.discharge_rate = - context.global.max_discharge_setpoint; // used in batt control as discharge setpoint for inverter
context.global.setpoint_adjusted = false;
context.global.max_phase_power = 5550; // max power (<25A) so a single phase does not get overloaded

context.global.batt_charge_rate = context.global.max_charge_setpoint * context.global.batt_charge_efficiency; // the net going into the batt
context.global.batt_discharge_rate = context.global.max_discharge_setpoint * context.global.batt_discharge_efficiency; // the net coming out of the batt

//From Tibber globals:
context.flow.price_min_sell_difference= 0.06; //6 cents
context.flow.price_percentage_sell_difference = 23; //%
context.flow.price_percentage_sell_efficiency = 0.8; //80%
context.flow.calculation_method = "price"; //price, percentage_difference, percentage_efficiency
context.flow.batt_min_charged = context.global.batt_capacity * context.global.min_soc / 100;
context.flow.batt_charged_capacity = context.global.batt_capacity * (context.global.max_soc - context.global.min_soc) / 100;
*/

